    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "<div class=\"interpretation\">\n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis suggests that the relationship between health expenditure and HIV prevalence is complex rather than simply inverse. Some countries with high HIV prevalence also have relatively high health expenditure because greater spending may reflect substantial investment in managing a large HIV burden.\n",
        "\n",
        "Countries with lower spending can also have lower prevalence for reasons unrelated to spending, including differences in epidemic history, population structure, prevention, testing, treatment coverage, and other social and health-system factors.\n",
        "\n",
        "</div> \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Conclusion \n",
        "\n",
        "<div class=\"conclusion-box\"> \n",
        "\n",
        "## Key Findings \n",
        "\n",
        "This analysis examined HIV prevalence among people aged 15–49 and current health expenditure per capita across countries and over time. \n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. HIV burden varies substantially \n",
        "\n",
        "HIV prevalence differs considerably between countries, with the highest levels concentrated in Southern and Eastern Africa. This reflects the historical development and geographical concentration of the HIV epidemic. \n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Health expenditure differs widely \n",
        "\n",
        "Current health expenditure per capita varies substantially across countries. Higher spending is generally concentrated in wealthier countries, while many low- and middle-income countries operate with considerably fewer resources per person. \n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Health spending and HIV prevalence have a complex relationship \n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis does not support a simple conclusion that higher health expenditure automatically results in lower HIV prevalence. Countries with high HIV prevalence may also have high health expenditure because substantial resources are required to provide HIV prevention, testing, treatment, and ARV therapy. \n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. Sustained investment remains important \n",
        "\n",
        "Continued investment in health systems, HIV prevention, testing, treatment, ARV therapy, and health-system capacity remains important for reducing HIV-related morbidity and improving long-term health outcomes. \n",
        "\n",
        "### Limitations \n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis is based on observational country-level data. Therefore, the observed relationships should be interpreted as associations rather than causal effects. Differences in epidemic history, socioeconomic conditions, healthcare access, prevention programmes, treatment coverage, and data quality may influence the results.\n",
        "\n",
        " </div> \n",
        " \n",
        " ## Final Message \n",
        " \n",
        " <div class=\"interpretation\"> \n",
        " \n",
        " **Overall, the dashboard demonstrates how publicly available health data can be combined with Python to identify patterns in HIV prevalence, health expenditure, and health-system investment. The findings highlight the importance of sustained HIV prevention, treatment, ARV therapy, and broader health-system strengthening.** \n",
        " \n",
        " </div>"
      ],
      "id": "fd4b252c"
    }
